## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of robust sorting strategies used in production libraries. Combines quicksort behavior, 3-way partitioning for duplicates, and a guaranteed worst-case fallback with heapsort. Assesses pivot choice, in-place constraints, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort with depth-based fallback to heapsort
- 3-way partitioning: <, =, > pivot to handle duplicates efficiently
- Depth limit: d = floor(2*log2(n)) prevents O(n^2) degeneration
- In-place implementation with O(log n) recursion overhead

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const depthLimit = n > 1 ? Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n)) : 0;

  function swap(i, j) {
    const t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = t;
  }

  function heapSortSub(start, end) {
    const len = end - start;
    function heapify(i, heapSize) {
      let largest = i;
      while (true) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1;
        const r = l + 1;
        let idx = i;
        if (l < heapSize && arr[start + l] > arr[start + idx]) idx = l;
        if (r < heapSize && arr[start + r] > arr[start + idx]) idx = r;
        if (idx !== i) { [arr[start + i], arr[start + idx]] = [arr[start + idx], arr[start + i]]; i = idx; } else break;
      }
    }
    for (let i = Math.floor(len / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) heapify(i, len);
    for (let endIdx = len - 1; endIdx > 0; endIdx--) {
      swap(start, start + endIdx);
      heapify(0, endIdx);
    }
  }

  function quicksort3(start, end, depth) {
    if (end - start <= 1) return;
    if (depth <= 0) {
      heapSortSub(start, end);
      return;
    }
    const pivot = arr[start + Math.floor((start + end - 1) / 2)];
    let lt = start, i = start, gt = end - 1;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { swap(lt, i); lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { swap(i, gt); gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    quicksort3(start, lt, depth - 1);
    quicksort3(gt + 1, end, depth - 1);
  }

  quicksort3(0, n, depthLimit);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for parallel sorting on large arrays?
- What changes if stability is required while keeping O(n log n) average time?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess understanding of Trie data structure and practical prefix search used in autocompletion; tests familiarity with basic data structures and iteration over strings.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with 26-way children
- insert/search/startsWith time complexity O(m)
- memory proportional to number of unique prefixes

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next = new Array(26).fill(null); this.end=false; } }
class Trie { constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); }
 insert(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) p.next[idx]=new TrieNode(); p=p.next[idx]; } p.end=true; }
 search(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return p.end; }
 startsWith(prefix){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return true; }
}
```

